// expect-exit: 94
//
// `Vec<T>` — OUT OF RANGE: `vec_free_owning` on an already-freed `Vec`.
//
// RX-144, and the worse half of the pair. `vec_free_owning` does not merely
// `dalloc`: it walks `0 .. count` MOVING each element out of the block and
// dropping it. Without the `cap <= 0` guard, a call after `vec_free` would run
// element destructors over memory the allocator has reclaimed, and only reach
// `dalloc`'s own check afterwards. Measured **exit 95, `Unreachable`** at
// `3d15ac9`.
//
// That it is currently survivable is an accident of `vec_free` also setting
// `count = 0`, which makes the loop body execute zero times. The guard does not
// rely on that: `count` is a field a caller can write, since a `pub struct` has
// no private fields (D-149), and the poison that matters is `cap`.
mod:vec_oob_free_owning_after_free;

use "../../src/core/vec.npk".*;

func:main = int32(cstring[]:argv) {
    Vec<int64>:v = raw vec_init::<int64>(4i64);
    drop vec_push(@v, 11i64);
    drop vec_free(@v);
    drop vec_free_owning(@v);       // cap is already 0

    exit 65i32;                     // reached only if neither call trapped
};

func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
    pick (e) {
        (HeapBadRequest) { exit 91i32; },
        (HeapOom)        { exit 92i32; },
        (IntOverflow)    { exit 93i32; },
        (OutOfBounds)    { exit 94i32; },
        (Unreachable)    { exit 95i32; },
        (WildLeak)       { exit 96i32; },
        (*)              { exit 99i32; }
    }
    exit 9i32;
};
